===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# Primarily physical senses.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To {{l|en|make}} physical {{l|en|contact}} with; to {{l|en|bring}} the hand, finger or other {{l|en|part}} of the body into contact with. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|contact|Thesaurus:feel}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''touched''' his {{l|en|face}} softly.}}
##* {{RQ:Landor Gebir|edition=2nd|page=107|passage=While thus she spake, / She '''toucht''' his eye-lashes with libant lip / And breath'd ambrosial odours; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To {{l|en|come}} into (involuntary) contact with; to {{l|en|meet}} or {{l|en|intersect}}. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Sitting on the {{l|en|bench}}, the {{l|en|hem}} of her {{l|en|skirt}} '''touched''' the ground.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come into physical contact, or to be in physical contact. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|They {{l|en|stood}} {{l|en|next}} to each other, their shoulders '''touching'''.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make physical contact with a thing. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Please can I have a {{l|en|look}}, if I promise not to '''touch'''?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To physically {{l|en|disturb}}; to interfere with, molest, or attempt to {{l|en|harm}} {{l|en|through}} contact. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|If you '''touch''' her, I'll kill you.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Genesis|27|28-29|passage=Let us make a covenant with thee, that thou {{l|en|wilt}} do us no {{l|en|hurt}}, as we have not '''touched''' thee.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[intimate]] physical contact with a person.
##: {{syn|en|caress|fondle|grope}}
##: {{ux|en|The man was arrested for '''touching''' a girl without her consent.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|or|reflexive}} To sexually excite with the fingers; to {{l|en|finger}} or {{l|en|masturbate}}. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Her parents had {{l|en|caught}} her '''touching''' herself when she was fifteen.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to be briefly in contact with something.
##: ''He quickly '''touched''' his knee to the worn marble.''
##: ''The demonstrator nearly '''touched''' the rod on the ball.''
##: ''She '''touched''' her lips to the glass.''
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To physically affect in specific ways {{l|en|implied}} by context. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Frankly, this {{l|en|wood}}'s so {{l|en|strong}} that sandpaper won't '''touch''' it.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To begin to {{l|en|consume}}, or otherwise use. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:ingest}}
##: {{ux|en|Are you all {{l|en|right}}? You've hardly '''touched''' your lunch.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=w:Georgette Heyer|title=w:The Unknown Ajax|chapter=1
|passage=But Richmond{{...}}appeared to {{l|en|lose}} himself in his own reflections. Some pickled crab, which he had not '''touched''', had been removed with a damson {{l|en|pie}}; and his sister saw{{...}}that he had eaten no more than a spoonful of that either.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a {{l|en|ship}} or its passengers: to {{l|en|land}}, to make a {{l|en|short}} {{l|en|stop}} (at). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|text=Now a {{l|en|certain}} {{l|en|grand}} merchant ship once '''touched''' at Rokovoko, and its commander &mdash; from all accounts, a very stately punctilious gentleman, at least for a sea captain &mdash; this commander was invited to the wedding {{l|en|feast}} of Queequeg's sister, a pretty young princess just turned of ten.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|now|historical}} To lay {{l|en|hands}} on (someone suffering from {{l|en|scrofula}}) as a {{l|en|form}} of {{l|en|cure}}, as formerly practised by English and French monarchs. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012|page=189
|passage=But in fact the English {{l|en|kings}} of the seventeenth century usually began to '''touch''' form the day of their accession, without waiting for any such consecration.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[fasten]]; to take effect; to make [[impression]].
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=Strong waters {{l|en|pierce}} metals, and will '''touch''' upon gold, that will not '''touch''' upon silver.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} To bring (a sail) so close to the {{l|en|wind}} that its weather leech shakes.
## {{lb|en|intransitive|nautical}} To be brought, as a {{l|en|sail}}, so {{l|en|close}} to the wind that its weather {{l|en|leech}} shakes.
## {{lb|en|nautical}} To keep the ship as near (the wind) as possible.
##: {{ux|en|to '''touch''' the wind}}
# Primarily non-physical senses.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To {{l|en|imbue}} or {{l|en|endow}} with a specific quality. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=09 Apr 88|journal=Gay Community News|page=6|author=Vicki Gabriner|title=Dancing for the Living and the Dead|text=This year, J. Allen Collier, the artistic director and producer, invited choreographers to create dance pieces exploring the multi-faceted responses to the AIDS crisis. This thematic unity '''touched''' the show with additional solemnity and grace.}}
##: {{ux|en|My grandfather, as many people know, was '''touched''' with greatness.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To deal with in {{l|en|speech}} or writing; to {{l|en|mention}} briefly, to {{l|en|allude}} to. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:mention}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd}}, I.2.4.vii:
##*: Next to sorrow still I may annex such accidents as procure {{l|en|fear}}; for besides those terrors which I have before '''touched''',{{...}}there is a superstitious fear{{...}}which much trouble many of us.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To deal with in speech or writing; briefly to speak or write (''on'' or ''upon'' something). {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|refer|Thesaurus:pertain}}
##* {{RQ:Stevenson Jekyll and Hyde|||"Well, but since we have '''touched''' upon this business, and for the last time I hope," continued the doctor, "there is one point I should like you to understand."}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To {{l|en|concern}}, to have to do with. {{defdate|14th|19th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Acts|V|text=Men of Israhell take hede to youreselves what ye entende to do as '''touchinge''' these men.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=The stories did not seem to me to '''touch''' life. They were plainly intended to have a bracing moral effect, and perhaps had this result for the people at whom they were aimed. They left me with the impression of a well-delivered stereopticon lecture, with characters about as life-like as the shadows on the screen, and whisking on and off, at the mercy of the operator.}}
##* '''1919''', {{w|Saki}}, ‘The Penance’, ''The Toys of Peace'', Penguin 2000 {{qualifier|Complete Short Stories}}, page 423:
##*: And now it seemed he was engaged in something which '''touched''' them closely, but must be hidden from their knowledge.
## {{senseid|en|affect emotionally}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To affect emotionally; to bring about {{l|en|tender}} or painful feelings in. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|affect|move|stir}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|IV|1|text=If you are so fond over her iniquity, give her patent<br>to offend, for if it '''touch''' not you, it comes near<br>nobody.}}
##: {{ux|en|Stefan was '''touched''' by the song's message of hope.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|dated}} To {{l|en|affect}} in a negative way, especially only slightly. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|He had been drinking over lunch, and was clearly '''touched'''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|Scottish history}} To give [[royal assent]] to by touching it with the [[sceptre]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The bill was finally '''touched''' after many hours of deliberation.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To obtain money from, usually by [[borrow]]ing (from a friend). {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|I was running short, so I '''touched''' old Bertie for a fiver.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|always passive}} To [[disturb]] the mental functions of; to make somewhat [[insane]]; often followed with "in the head". {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|dement|Thesaurus:becraze}}
##: {{ux|en|You must be '''touched''' if you think I'm taking your advice.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|in negative constructions}} To be on the {{l|en|level}} of; to approach in excellence or quality. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{syn|en|match|rival|Thesaurus:be equal}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1928|author=w:Dorothy L. Sayers|chapter=The Abominable History of the Man with Copper Fingers|title=w:Lord Peter Views the Body
|passage=There was his mistress, Maria Morano. I don't think I've ever seen anything to '''touch''' her, and when you work for the screen [as I do] you're apt to have a pretty exacting standard of female beauty.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Agatha Christie|title=Murder on the Orient Express|location=London|publisher=HarperCollins|year=1934|year_published= 2017|chapter=6|page=118|passage='Lind Arden was a great genius, one of the greatest tragic actresses in the world. As Lady Macbeth, as Magda, there was no one to '''touch''' her.'}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To come close to; to [[approach]].
##: {{syn|en|near}}
##* {{quote-text|en|date=July 15 2012|author=Richard Williams|publisher=Guardian Unlimited|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2012/jul/15/tour-de-france-2012-bradley-wiggins|title=Tour de France 2012: Carpet tacks cannot force Bradley Wiggins off track
|passage=On Sunday afternoon it was as dark as night, with barely room for two riders abreast on a gradient that '''touches''' 20%.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To mark (a file or document) as having been modified.
# To [[try]]; to [[prove]], as with a {{l|en|touchstone}}.
#: {{syn|en|test}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|act=III|scene=iii|passage=I mean to '''touch''' your love indeed.}}
# To mark or delineate with touches; to add a slight stroke to with the pencil or brush.
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Criticism|passage=The lines, though '''touched''' but faintly, are drawn right.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To {{l|en|infect}}; to {{l|en|affect}} slightly.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7|passage=Hee was '''touched''' with great Remorse}}
# To [[strike]]; to [[manipulate]]; to [[play]] on.
#: {{ux|en|to '''touch''' an instrument of music}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=7|passage=[They] '''touched''' their golden harps.|year=1873}}
# To [[perform]], as a tune; to [[play]].
#* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|passage=A person in the royal retinue '''touched''' a light and lively air on the flageolet.}}
# To [[influence]] by [[impulse]]; to [[impel]] forcibly.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=10|passage=No decree of mine,{{...}}[to] '''touch''' with lightest moment of impulse his free will.|year=1873}}

